            <ArticleTitle>Cervical spinal epidural abscess following acupuncture and wet-cupping therapy: A case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>108-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.ctim.2015.12.002</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0965-2299(15)30023-6</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">Report of an uncommon complication of acupuncture and wet cupping.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 54-year-old man presented with neck pain and fever. Magnetic resonance imaging of the cervical spine revealed an epidural abscess at C4 to T2.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The symptoms related to epidural abscess resolved partially after treatment with antibiotics.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Acupuncture and wet-cupping therapy should be taken into consideration as a cause of spinal epidural abscesses in patients who present with neck pain and fever. Furthermore, acupuncture and wet-cupping practitioners should pay attention to hygienic measures.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Copyright © 2015 Elsevier Ltd. All rights reserved.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
